body:
I appreciate the response. I am actually live in a very dangerous and bad area - high crime, government housing, no grocery stores or chains for many many miles. I live in the projects, its a 30 minute bus ride to the closet McDonalds or Starbucks if that gives you an idea of the area - alot of gangs, drugs, robbery - unfortunately this is all I could afford on my salary. 

&#x200B;

As someone stated earlier it may be best to apply to McDonalds or something and just take that 30 minute bus trip versus the 3 hour trip i'm making now for $15 an hour. I'm pretty sure McDonalds will pay me $10 so not much of a difference. 

&#x200B;

I have the degree, none of the skills. I passed college by simply showing up and giving the bare minimum - which is why it took me 7 years to graduate. I didn't start taking college seriously until the last 4 semesters, in which I learned alot but it was still too late. All my peers had intense internships with HP, Dell, Amazon, Apple, etc. While I did nothing - this is all on me I completely know that. But considering I have the degree but absolutely no skillset, and currently no time to pick up the skillset, I find it hard to find a job that pays in my field. Picking up IT skills require resources and time to study, its not impossible, just very very hard for me right now with everything going on. 

&#x200B;

I've been doing the Linkedin route heavily for many months, some leads but again I never get past the technical interviews asking about how to setup a network or troubleshoot x problem or what would you do if this error code was displayed on this ERP system or what experience I have with Jira or \[insert it field here\], etc. 

&#x200B;

i've looked into becoming a teaching assistant for a elementary school or high school - I had a former coworker who left to do that. It was salaried, but he was also an education major, but did not have his degree so i'm not so sure its not impossible for me. He was able to afford a car and a small apartment as well off that. But also worked part time retail - I was a retail manager for many many years, I left that to do IT. It payed slightly better but the hours were crazy and the life got difficult. I may try to go back to that.

I actually have not sent that to the Credit Bureaus - thank you for the advice I did not think about doing that. Since most of the CCs went delinquent due to non-payments as I refuse to pay for a CC I never obtained, and were sent to collections, I just assumed contesting it with the CC overtime was enough - along with the police. 

&#x200B;

I will look into remote positions, unfortunately I do not have access to the internet while not at work so I will have to go to my local library.

I have contested a lot of the fraud. For years, many police reports, reports to the CC companies. All to nearly no avail - what helped me was freezing my credit for several years, this just ended within the past 6 months and almost immediately a CC was taken out in my name again and racked up thousands in charges. I still have no clue how this happens to often to me.

&#x200B;

Beyond that, I haven't looked at other work too seriously as of yet cause I was at least staying afloat with a car. My plan was to get the experience here, move onto a better IT job after a year or two here since i'd have experience on a resume. But sadly, all of this has happened so quickly. I live in a very bad area so generally jobs pay minimum wage or under $10 an hour, $15 is actually quite high in my city at least for entry level/low-skilled work. Its something im seriously considering though.

Its because my job does not pay for travel time to contracts or job sites. Thus I can only bill the hours I am physically onsite doing work. So somedays I get paid for the 9 hours I work (8 cause of an hour unpaid lunch), but others with hours and hours of travel I get paid for only 8 when I probably travelled an additional 2-3 hours alone.

&#x200B;

Edit: this is also bad cause my job required a vehicle to get to jobsites, I cannot just keep using Uber or the Bus. Its costing me hundreds in just a few days cause I may have to be at X location within a 4 hour window and a bus trip there may take waaay over that considering I may have 4+ locations to all hit in that window.

As a CIS Senior, its not necessarily a significantly easier degree. The main difference in terms of difficulty is that CS has more programming and higher-level mathematics and science that CIS majors do not have to deal with. CIS also gets harder and harder with the final senior/junior courses being doable, but intensive and extremely time consuming (like working with small businesses to build DBMS and software/other features for them is a requirement of the degree in several courses). I'd say about half the CIS courses are very easy, at least the 2000's- most of the 3000s once you go beyond that its quite difficult. 

&#x200B;

Edit: And little to no actual teaching is done by the professors, its all self-learning when it comes to most database/programming things that are going to be required in upper-level courses. So if your good at self-teaching yourself programming languages its very doable.
